REALROM sample.note e2e8 [NesBankingAnalyzer] Bank value is this helper's ARGUMENT: the backward scan reached the helper's entry, where the register holds whatever the caller supplied, so the bank cannot be known from inside the helper body. Each CALL SITE is resolved separately; see the call sites' own annotations.
REALROM sample.note e5bc [NesBankingAnalyzer] Bank value is RESTORED here, not resolved: this call re-commits the bank READ BACK at e5af from bank-identifying ROM byte 8000 -- a byte that, committed back to the bank register as read, re-selects a bank whose window image is identical to the one it is read from (byte re-selects the same bank image), so the bank committed is the bank live at the read -- that the live bank is one this was verified on is PROVEN by the tracked state at the read, carried on the stack across the call at e5b5 -- the save/restore idiom. The bank after this call is the bank 8000 identified at that read, not a fresh value this analyzer failed to pin down. That read sits at the ENTRY of FUN_e5af (nothing switches between its entry and the read), so it is the bank live on entry: each of its call sites' own bank, which those sites already track -- the call passes it through unchanged. A property of the game, not a gap in analysis.
